WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:02.705
Catastrophe at Sea In the evening of March 23, 1989, a huge oil tanker called the Exxon Valdez

2
00:00:02.706 --> 00:00:13.362
left the dock at Port Valdez, Alaska. The tanker's final destination was Long Beach, California, where it

3
00:00:13.363 --> 00:00:21.701
was scheduled to deliver about 54 million gallons of oil. On the way the tanker would pass through

4
00:00:21.702 --> 00:00:30.853
Prince William Sound, a beautiful but dangerous body of water on the southern coast of Alaska. Soon

5
00:00:30.854 --> 00:00:40.180
after the ship left the port, pilot William Murphy guided the vessel through the Valdez Narrows, a narrow

6
00:00:40.181 --> 00:00:52.350
waterway that led into the Sound. At around eleven o'clock, Captain Joseph Hazelwood relieved Murphy, increasing the

7
00:00:52.351 --> 00:01:02.091
speed of the ship and steering it out of the usual shipping lanes in order to avoid icebergs.

8
00:01:02.092 --> 00:01:12.251
Just before midnight Hazelwood left Third Mate Gregory Cousins alone on the bridge of the ship, a

9
00:01:12.252 --> 00:01:17.148
clear violation of company rules. Cousins, who had already been working for more hours than was allowed, was

10
00:01:17.149 --> 00:01:20.648
now on his own and in charge of maneuvering the tanker back into the proper lane. As

11
00:01:20.649 --> 00:01:27.475
Hazelwood had instructed, Cousins gave the order to turn the ship. But the heavy tanker didn't respond quickly

12
00:01:27.476 --> 00:01:30.388
enough. Instead it headed straight for Bligh Reef, a cluster of jagged, underwater rocks. Cousins phoned Hazelwood

13
00:01:30.389 --> 00:01:39.877
in a panic. The two men were still on the phone seconds later when there was a series

14
00:01:39.878 --> 00:01:45.637
of violent jolts, and the tanker grounded on top of Bligh Reef. The sharp rocks sliced through

15
00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:52.882
the ship, ripping open its steel cargo holds. Thick, black oil began pouring into the Sound—nearly 11 million

16
00:01:52.883 --> 00:01:59.200
gallons of it. The 1989 Exxon Valdez oil spill created a massive environmental crisis. The result of

17
00:01:59.201 --> 00:02:07.439
human error, the accident damaged one of the most beautiful and unspoiled wilderness environments in the world. By

18
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:16.774
the time it was all over, 460 miles of ocean water and 1,300 miles of the Alaskan

19
00:02:16.775 --> 00:02:23.011
coastline were contaminated. Hundreds of thousands of animals were killed, and the lives of many were changed forever.

20
00:02:23.012 --> 00:02:25.416
A Delicate Ecosystem Prince William Sound was famous for its clear, pristine waters that teemed with wildlife—fish,

21
00:02:25.417 --> 00:02:37.685
otters, sea lions, porpoises, whales, and other marine creatures. Surrounding the sea inlet was a huge forest, with

22
00:02:37.686 --> 00:02:48.750
towering mountains that were home to grizzly bears, bald eagles, and many species that depended on the

23
00:02:48.751 --> 00:02:58.151
Sound for food. Humans living nearby were also dependent on the water, making their living from fishing salmon,

24
00:02:58.152 --> 00:03:06.145
herring, mussels, and clams. When the Exxon Valdez grounded on Bligh Reef, the oil that spilled into

25
00:03:06.146 --> 00:03:14.470
the water damaged the area's delicate ecosystem. The oil poisoned animals that ingested it, and it coated their

26
00:03:14.471 --> 00:03:21.216
skin and feathers so they could no longer move or keep warm. Within the first few days

27
00:03:21.217 --> 00:03:32.871
of the spill, about 250,000 seabirds were found dead. It is believed that thousands of other animals—sea otters,

28
00:03:32.872 --> 00:03:41.118
harbor seals, killer whales, salmon, and herring—also died within the first days after the spill. The oil

29
00:03:41.119 --> 00:03:43.610
killed even the tiniest organisms, from algae growing on shoreline rocks to plankton living in the water. Rescue

30
00:03:43.611 --> 00:03:50.279
and Recovery Cleanup crews were immediately sent to Alaska to begin the process of restoring the water

31
00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.208
and land. But from the start, this was a difficult process. Workers first tried to burn off the

32
00:03:54.209 --> 00:04:03.522
oil—a common means of dealing with oil spills—but high winds and rain prevented them from making much

33
00:04:03.523 --> 00:04:09.672
progress. Because of the Sound's remote location, it took a long time for essential equipment to arrive. Chemicals

34
00:04:09.673 --> 00:04:17.425
were deposited in the water to help break up the oil, but there was not enough wave

35
00:04:17.426 --> 00:04:22.161
action to make this process successful. For the first two days, the spill was contained around Bligh Reef.

36
00:04:22.162 --> 00:04:34.165
But when a storm arrived on March 26, high winds and strong waves pushed the oil further

37
00:04:34.166 --> 00:04:37.670
out to sea. Soon oil was washing up along the beaches too. Cleaning the beaches created a new

38
00:04:37.671 --> 00:04:43.333
set of problems. One of the most effective methods was to use high-pressure, hot-water hoses. But scientists

39
00:04:43.334 --> 00:04:55.562
soon realized that the water had a terrible side effect: it was so hot that it cooked microorganisms,

40
00:04:55.563 --> 00:05:03.976
such as plankton, that were important for the ecosystem. A newer cleanup method, bioremediation, involved using fertilizer

41
00:05:03.977 --> 00:05:08.555
to grow bacteria that consumed oil. But this technique was very new so it was used sparingly. And

42
00:05:08.556 --> 00:05:13.878
it was only effective in locations where the oil had not spread too thickly. Meanwhile tens of

43
00:05:13.879 --> 00:05:22.194
thousands of volunteers were working around the clock to try to save the animals affected by the oil.

44
00:05:22.195 --> 00:05:30.281
This was difficult work too. First, the animals, who were frightened and sick, had to be caught.

45
00:05:30.282 --> 00:05:40.521
Then, they were brought to special facilities for the painstaking task of cleaning them one-by-one, using dish soap

46
00:05:40.522 --> 00:05:46.251
and toothbrushes. The animals were also given medicine to prevent the oil from damaging their internal organs.

47
00:05:46.252 --> 00:05:53.101
Volunteers had to work quickly so that the animals could be released back into the wild as soon

48
00:05:53.102 --> 00:06:00.427
as possible. Four years later the massive cleanup operation around Prince William Sound was finally declared over.

49
00:06:00.428 --> 00:06:08.069
Cleanup crews left the area, but unfortunately a lot of the oil remained behind on the beaches and

50
00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:10.180
in the water. After the Crisis In 1991 the Exxon Corporation, the company that owned the Exxon

51
00:06:10.181 --> 00:06:23.476
Valdez , agreed to pay more than $1 billion to help people and the environment recover from the

52
00:06:23.477 --> 00:06:31.160
accident. Some of that money was put in a special fund to purchase land in the area

53
00:06:31.161 --> 00:06:41.240
so that it could be preserved for wildlife. Hazelwood, the captain of the tanker, was fired, and later

54
00:06:41.241 --> 00:06:46.292
he was fined and ordered to perform one thousand hours of community service. Today Prince William Sound

55
00:06:46.293 --> 00:06:54.136
still bears scars from the spill. As of 2007 there were still over 25,000 gallons of oil contained

56
00:06:54.137 --> 00:07:00.221
in the shoreline sand. Some bird populations, such as pigeon guillemots, have shown little sign of recovery.

57
00:07:00.222 --> 00:07:07.862
The decrease in fish populations, especially herring, has created many financial difficulties for local fishermen. However, there are

58
00:07:07.863 --> 00:07:12.027
also some small signs of hope. The crisis taught scientists and emergency workers important lessons about the

59
00:07:12.028 --> 00:07:19.765
best ways to clean up oil spills. The U.S government passed the Oil Pollution Act (OPA), which created

60
00:07:19.766 --> 00:07:29.433
strict regulations for oil tankers traveling around Alaska. It also made owners of tankers more directly responsible

61
00:07:29.434 --> 00:07:35.837
for cleaning up any accidents. Perhaps the best news is that some of the animal species affected by

62
00:07:35.838 --> 00:07:41.667
the spill are recovering. Pink salmon, river otters, common loons, and bald eagles are considered recovered, and

63
00:07:41.668 --> 00:07:53.586
the number of killer whales and sea otters in Prince William Sound seems to be rising. For some

64
00:07:53.587 --> 00:07:59.998
people the crisis has provided encouraging proof of nature's ability to heal itself. It appears that over

65
00:07:59.999 --> 00:08:08.476
time the natural movement of the waves has washed away more oil than any of the human methods.

